{% doc %}
  blocks/_featured-pills-carousel.liquid

  A slideshow inside one **Pill** panel: a run of slides and the
  `( ← ) 01 ▬▬▬▬▭▭▭ 05 ( → )` bar that walks them.

  ## None of the carousel is this file's

  The element is `<carousel-slider>` from `assets/carousel.js`, the attributes
  and the options JSON are `snippets/carousel-shell-attrs.liquid`, the pause
  button and the live region are `carousel-shell-tail.liquid`, and the control
  bar is `carousel-controls.liquid` — which draws exactly the arrangement the
  *Ingredients* panel was drawn with, zero-padded numbers and all, because that
  bar is what the snippet is.

  The whole `carousel_*` settings group is the one every other carousel in the
  theme carries, id for id, so a merchant who has set up the Feature cards
  carousel has set this one up.

  ## Why the element is written out here rather than through `carousel-shell`

  Because its slides are theme blocks. `{% content_for 'blocks' %}` cannot have
  its output captured — Shopify renders blocks with their editor bindings
  attached and buffering that is not something the platform supports — so a
  caller whose slides are blocks has to own the tag and let the two half
  snippets supply what goes before and after the track.
  `sections/feature-cards.liquid` and `sections/multicolumn.liquid` do the same,
  for the same reason, and the comment there is the long version of this one.

  ## The bar sits on the picture, or under it

  **Over the slides** is the drawn design: the bar floats inside the frame near
  the bottom edge, on a lozenge of its own. It is absolutely positioned, so it
  takes no room and a slide's height is the slide's business.

  **Under the slides** puts it back in the flow for a carousel whose slides are
  not photographs, where a control floating over content is just a control in
  the way.

  Either way it is *detached* — rendered outside `<carousel-slider>` and linked
  back by `data-carousel-for` — because a bar positioned against the frame
  cannot also be a child of the thing that clips to it.

  ## Private, and this section's

  A slideshow is not a scarce component; the theme has carousels everywhere. It
  is private because its *slides* are: `_featured-pills-slide` is a picture that
  holds this section's own blocks, and a slide outside a track is a
  `.carousel-shell__item` with nothing to slide in.
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign s = block.settings

  comment
    One slide at a time is the drawn design and the default, but the columns are
    settings rather than a constant: three ingredient cards in a row that scroll
    together is the same block, and a section that could only ever show one
    would need a second one for that.
  endcomment
  assign cols_desktop = s.columns_desktop | default: 1 | plus: 0
  assign cols_tablet = s.columns_tablet | default: cols_desktop | plus: 0
  assign cols_mobile = s.columns_mobile | default: 1 | plus: 0

  comment
    Always a carousel, at both breakpoints. `carousel-shell-attrs` takes the
    grid/carousel pair because most callers offer the choice; this one does not
    — a slideshow that is a grid on a desktop is a grid, and the section already
    has blocks for those.
  endcomment
  assign carousel_id = 'FeaturedPillsCarousel-' | append: block.id

  assign placement = s.controls_placement | default: 'over'

  comment
    `controls` is one bar carrying its own arrows, so rendering the bare pair
    beside it would draw the navigation twice — the same test
    `carousel-shell-tail.liquid` makes when the controls are nested.
  endcomment
  assign pagination = s.carousel_pagination | default: 'controls'
  assign pagination_align = s.carousel_pagination_align | default: 'center'

  assign show_nav = s.carousel_navigation
  if show_nav == nil
    assign show_nav = true
  endif

  assign classes = 'featured-pills-carousel featured-pills-carousel--' | append: placement
-%}

{%- capture wrap_style -%}
  {%- render 'spacing', block: s, declarations: true -%}
  --fpx-gap: {{ s.gap | default: 16 | plus: 0 }}px;
  --fpx-radius: {{ s.border_radius | default: 28 | plus: 0 }}px;
  {%- if placement == 'over' -%}
    --fpx-controls-inset: {{ s.controls_inset | default: 24 | plus: 0 }}px;
    --fpx-controls-radius: {{ s.controls_radius | default: 50 | plus: 0 }}px;
    --fpx-controls-padding: {{ s.controls_padding | default: 8 | plus: 0 }}px;
    {%- if s.controls_background != blank -%}
      --fpx-controls-background: {{ s.controls_background }};
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- comment -%}
  `kind: 'image'` and not `'text'`: a slide is a photograph, and the text
  family's presets split their target into words and letters — which on a
  picture is nothing at all, and on the card over it is the card coming apart.
{%- endcomment -%}
<motion-effect
  class="{{ classes }}"
  data-block-spacing
  style="{{ wrap_style | strip_newlines | strip }}"
  {% render 'motion-attrs', kind: 'image', block: s %}
  {{ block.shopify_attributes }}
>
  <carousel-slider
    {% render 'carousel-shell-attrs',
       settings: s,
       id: carousel_id,
       layout: 'carousel',
       layout_mobile: 'carousel',
       classes: 'featured-pills-carousel__shell',
       columns_desktop: cols_desktop,
       columns_tablet: cols_tablet,
       columns_mobile: cols_mobile %}
  >
    <div
      class="carousel-shell__track featured-pills-carousel__track"
      data-ref="track"
      aria-label="{{ s.label | default: 'Slideshow' | escape }}"
    >
      {% content_for 'blocks' %}
    </div>

    {%- comment -%}
      `detached` keeps the pause button and the live region — both are found by
      ref and announced from inside the element — and stands the arrows and the
      pagination down, so the bar below is the only one drawn.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {% render 'carousel-shell-tail',
       settings: s,
       layout: 'carousel',
       layout_mobile: 'carousel',
       detached: true %}
  </carousel-slider>

  <div class="featured-pills-carousel__controls">
    {%- if pagination == 'controls' -%}
      {% render 'carousel-controls',
         settings: s,
         carousel: carousel_id,
         align: pagination_align %}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- if show_nav -%}
        {% render 'carousel-nav',
           settings: s,
           carousel: carousel_id,
           position: 'bottom' %}
      {%- endif -%}

      {%- if pagination != 'none' -%}
        {% render 'carousel-pagination',
           style: pagination,
           align: pagination_align,
           carousel: carousel_id %}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</motion-effect>

{%- comment -%}
  Every setting below is a control a merchant reaches in the editor, and the
  schema is one element's styling surface: splitting it to get under Theme
  Check's count of 40 would split the thing the settings style.
{%- endcomment -%}
